        "semianalysis.com"
      ],
      "_note": "AI x politics, on three axes only: (1) POLICY/REGULATION - AI acts, executive orders, copyright and liability litigation, national AI strategies, safety and privacy rulemaking, procurement rules; (2) AI IN ELECTIONS/PUBLIC OPINION - deepfakes, synthetic campaign media, bot-driven astroturfing, platform labelling and provenance rules, political communication; (3) AI GEOPOLITICS - chip export controls, US-China AI competition, sovereign AI programs, datacenter siting diplomacy, compute and talent flows. OUT OF SCOPE: general political news with no AI axis (elections, personnel, party politics as such). Relevance test: AI must be what makes the story happen - if you can delete AI from the headline and it still stands, drop it. NEUTRALITY (required): report what was decided/filed/published and what it changes for people who build with AI. No partisan framing, no endorsement or criticism of parties or candidates, no horse-race or polling commentary. Attribute contested claims to the named party rather than asserting them. Prefer the primary document (bill text, ruling, agency release) over commentary about it. RUNNING STORIES: politics stories continue for days (ruling -> appeal -> suit). A follow-up on an already-published story is a duplicate unless it reports a new decision, ruling, filing, bill, executive order or official release; commentary, analysis, reactions and extra reporting on a published event are dropped. The primary document and the news report about it are ONE story - publish one of them. Crawler-blocked/paywalled majors (reuters.com, bloomberg.com, ft.com, wsj.com, nytimes.com, economist.com, foreignpolicy.com) deliberately excluded - published times are unverifiable there; the outlets above usually carry the same decision with a readable timestamp."
    },
